Type erasure — what the java virtual machine actually sees.

Everything so far happens at compile time. At run time, Java generics largely disappear — a process called type erasure. And understanding it turns a whole pile of confusing "why can't I do this?" errors into a single, predictable rule.

So what does erasure do? When the compiler is done checking your generic code, it erases the type parameters, and compiles down to code that uses the bound — or Object, if the parameter is unbounded — inserting casts where needed. So you write a list of string, add "hi", and read index zero into a string. But what the JVM effectively runs is a plain, raw list; it adds "hi"; and it reads index zero with an inserted cast to string. A list of string becomes a plain list; angle-T becomes Object; angle-T-extends-number becomes Number. The generic type information is used to verify your code, and then thrown away. The casts that old pre-generics code used to make by hand are still there — the compiler just writes them for you now, and guarantees they'll never fail.

Here's the one consequence that explains all the rest: all instantiations of a generic type share one class at run time. A list of string and a list of integer are the same class — just list. In fact, if you take the get-class of a new array-list of string, and of a new array-list of integer, they're equal. It's true. There is no list-of-string type in the running JVM — only list. And every restriction below follows from that single fact: the run-time system simply doesn't know the type argument.

So, what can you therefore not do at run time? First, no T-dot-class, and no list-of-string-dot-class. A class literal needs a real run-time type, and the type parameter has none — though the raw list-dot-class is fine. Second, no instanceof list-of-string. The check can't see the argument, so only instanceof list-of-question-mark, or the raw list, is allowed. Third, no new T, and no new T array — the run time doesn't know which class to instantiate; you pass a supplier of T, or a class-of-T token, when you truly need to create one. And fourth, you can't have two overloads that differ only by type argument — a method taking a list of string, and one taking a list of integer, both erase to the same signature, taking a raw list, which is a compile error. None of these are arbitrary; each is just "the run time doesn't have the type argument", wearing a different hat.

Why did Java choose erasure? It was a backward-compatibility decision. Generics arrived in Java five, long after millions of lines of raw-list code already existed. Erasure let generic and legacy code interoperate on the very same list class — a list of string and an old raw list are the same type at run time, so both keep working. The price is the restrictions we just covered, and the loss of run-time type information. Languages that reify generics — that keep the argument at run time, like C sharp — avoid these gotchas, but they couldn't have made that migration.

So, the practical upshot, in two habits. When you need a type at run time, pass a class-of-T token — the pattern behind enum-set dot all-of, and JSON parsers — because the type parameter itself is gone. And when the compiler emits an "unchecked" warning, understand what it's telling you: "I can't verify this cast survives erasure — you're vouching for it." That warning, and the array-and-cast gotchas it flags, are the whole of the next section.
